/a></div></div><div class="StoryHero_caption__eOw9W"><div>Metro Council recently learned that the Strobel House, Nashville&#x27;s first permanant housing for the homeless, had been operating without a contract.</div></div></div></div><div class="StoryPage_storyTextBlockContainer__LUkwY"><div class="StoryControls_controls__pSYQ_ StoryPage_controlsContainer__StRFl"><div class="Comment_commentWrapper__cGs3_ StoryPage_controlsContainer__StRFl"><div class="Comment_bubbleContainer__I9Tld"><button aria-label="Comment on this story" class="BaseButton_baseButton__PZJj9" type="button"><div class="BaseButton_buttonContent__3ERR7 Comment_commentGap__lJbAn"><span class="BaseButton_icon__9pZM8"><svg height="28px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 42 42" width="28px" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><title>Comment on this story</title><g fill="none" fill-rule="evenodd" id="Page-1" stroke="none" stroke-width="1"><g fill="#FFFFFF" id="L-|-Story-Detail---Commenting" stroke="#121212" transform="translate(-214.000000, -907.000000)"><g id="Group" transform="translate(215.000000, 908.000000)"><path d="M7.30803419,33.8461538 C5.25675214,33.8461538 3.52547009,33.1897436 2.11692308,31.874188 C0.705641026,30.5613675 0,28.9422222 0,27.0194872 L0,6.92239316 C0,4.99965812 0.705641026,3.36410256 2.11692308,2.01846154 C3.52547009,0.672820513 5.25675214,0 7.30803419,0 L32.6919658,0 C34.7432479,0 36.4745299,0.672820513 37.885812,2.01846154 C39.294359,3.36410256 40,4.99965812 40,6.92239316 L40,27.0194872 C40,28.9422222 39.294359,30.5613675 37.885812,31.874188 C36.4745299,33.1897436 34.7432479,33.8461538 32.6919658,33.8461538 L32.3090598,33.8461538 L32.3090598,40 C27.2437607,36.5374359 24.4868376,34.6475214 24.0382906,34.3275214 C23.5897436,34.0075214 22.9169231,33.8461538 22.0198291,33.8461538 L7.30803419,33.8461538 Z" id="sbg-comment"></path></g></g></g></svg><span class="Comment_commentCount__I6yAX">1</span></span><span class="BaseButton_label__jyklb Comment_commentLabel__cnGR6">Comment</span></div></button></div></div><button aria-label="Share story" class="BaseButton_baseButton__PZJj9 StoryPage_controlsContainer__StRFl" type="button"><div class="BaseButton_buttonContent__3ERR7"><span class="BaseButton_icon__9pZM8"><svg height="22px" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 27 22" width="27px" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"><title>Share story</title><g fill="none" fill-rule="evenodd" id="Design-V6" stroke="none" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="1"><g id="UI-Elements" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="1.2" transform="translate(-174, -226)"><g id="Group-7-Copy-3" transform="translate(174, 227)"><g id="Share" transform="translate(1.5, 0)"><path d="M24.000048,7.19038119 L13.9941255,0 L13.9941255,3.62430582 C-0.889019116,3.62430582 0.0104599993,20 0.0104599993,20 C0.0104599993,20 2.19550555,10.7564566 13.9941255,10.7564566 L13.9941255,14.3807624 L24.000048,7.19038119 Z" id="Stroke-1"></path></g></g></g></g></svg></span><span class="BaseButton_label__jyklb">Share</span></div></button></div><div class="StoryPage_contentColumnContainer__fMqAt"><div class="StoryPage_taboolaDeeperDive__WKVX7" data-url="https://fox17.com/news/local/metro-council-cites-lack-of-oversight-after-contract-lapse-at-homeless-housing-facility" id="deeper-dive-container-0"></div><div class="TopicsBar_topicsBar__eU8Uv StoryPage_topicsContainer__YIZ_i"><ul class="TopicsBar_topicsContainer__GzlWc"><h4 class="TopicsBar_title__YlW20">topics: </h4><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Oversight">Oversight</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Strobel%20House">Strobel House</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Metro%20Council">Metro Council</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Homeless%20services">Homeless services</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Depaul%20USA">Depaul USA</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Contract%20lapse">Contract lapse</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Funding%20sources">Funding sources</a></li><li class="TopicsBar_topic__8UfR4"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TopicsBar_aTag__uumvE" href="/topic/Financial%20safeguards">Financial safeguards</a></li></ul></div><div class="StoryPage_divider__ZF7oD"></div><div class="StoryText_storyText__C3hWI StoryText_storyText__C3hWI"><div><p><span class="dateline">NASHVILLE, Tenn. (WZTV) — </span>Nashville’s first permanent supportive housing facility for people experiencing homelessness just marked its first anniversary, but with  it came a cloud of serious administrative oversight.</p><p>Metro Council recently learned that<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xh3P4yBZjr4" target="_blank"> the Strobel House</a>, which currently houses and provides services for 90 residents, had been operating without a contract for nearly a year. The facility’s agreement with management company Depaul USA expired last September, according to Metro Department of Finance officals. But Metro’s Office of Homeless Services (OHS) continued payments through other funding sources, including vouchers, bypassing the finance department and reducing oversight.</p><p>“This is egregious. It shouldn’t have happened,” said Councilmember Zulfat Suara,  during Metro Council&#x27;s last meeting on Aug. 19.</p><div class="PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_mobile__XQxD4 PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_primisWrapper__G3UAH"><div class="PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_advertisementTag__2bPJb"></div><div id="primis-mid-story-ad-0-anchor-mobile"></div></div><p>“There’s a huge lack of oversight,” said Councilmember Ginny Welsch.</p><p>The lack of a formal agreement required Metro Council to take swift action. Council members approved a $3.5 million grant contract to keep the Strobel House operational. The first payment is set to go out this week, on Sept. 5. </p><div class="PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_desktop__dq_iB PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_primisWrapper__G3UAH"><div class="PrimisMidArticleAdUnit_advertisementTag__2bPJb"></div><div id="primis-mid-story-ad-0-anchor-desktop"></div></div><p>OHS Director April Calvin pointed to multiple contributing factors, including the complexity of the project, staffing turnover, and coordination across several Metro departments.</p><p>“The complexity of the problem... the complexity of the project. This was a one-of-a-kind for Metro, first-of-its-kind for our Office of Homeless Services,” Calvin said to Metro Council&#x27;s Budget and Finance Committee. </p><p>But not everyone was satisfied with the explanation.</p><p>“I&#x27;m looking for who can take over the finances of this whole thing because we are out of order,” said Mike Lacy, who spoke during the meeting&#x27;s public comment period. </p><p>“This is not at all a unique situation with OHS. We have found errors in contracts, math errors- things we’ve had to correct,” Welsch said.</p><p>In response to the oversight, OHS outlined steps it’s taking to prevent issues in the future, including hiring a dedicated contract administrator, increasing training for employees, and improving interdepartmental coordination and tracking.</p><p>“I think this points to some very fundamental flaws in how we are doing our finance and oversight within Metro,” Welsch said. </p><p>The situation has raised broader concerns about whether Metro Nashville needs stronger financial safeguards across departments.</p><p>The Strobel House has operated for more than a year, helping Nashville transition people without housing to more permanent stability. OHS reported some residents have enrolled or graduated from college since joining the center. Others have celebrated months sober. </p><p>In the last two years, OHS reported it moved 3,868 people in Nashville from homelessness to housing. </p></div></div></div></div></div><div class="TaboolaMoreToExplore_taboolaContainerWrapper__Ccf_j"><div id="taboola-7b52b505-4d38-4eae-a970-435c1f4dae5d"></div></div><div aria-hidden="true" class="StoryCommentingSection_lazyLoadSentinel__POpRG" data-testid="openweb-lazy-load-sentinel" id="js-openWebConversation-Container_0" style="height:1px;width:100%"></div></div></div><div class="StickyTwoColumn_rightColumn__vCmRf"><div class="StoryPage_rightColumn__t9tAM"><div class="Panel_panel__wT2cw"><div class="Breakpoints_fallbackWrapperDefault__4Pzjc Breakpoints_display-XS-only__cyBqQ Breakpoints_display-S-only__HbxV8 Breakpoints_display-M-only__ABnKP Breakpoints_display-L-only__s1GQt Breakpoints_display-XL-only__hEcw3"><div style="height:681px"></div></div></div><div class="TeasersBlockSidebar_teaserBlock__wUrKC Panel_panel__wT2cw"><h2 class="TeasersBlockSidebar_teasersTitle__hJZ_x">Trending</h2><div class="TeasersBlockSidebar_teasers__swsOT"><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TeaserLink_teaserLink__p1kuV TeaserLink_sidebarPrimary__CoLUx" href="/news/local/family-reaches-11m-settlement-over-babys-brain-injury-at-fort-campbell-army-hospital?teaserSource=trending"><picture class="TeaserLink_teaserPicture__kW5wk"><img alt="" aria-hidden="true" fetchPriority="auto" height="197" loading="lazy" src="/resources/media2/16x9/1786/648/25x0/90/305899a8-6d51-4692-900d-68aed628364b-BABYKARENPHOTO1.png" width="351"/></picture><div class="TeaserLink_teaserText__g1DQc"><div>Family reaches $11M settlement over baby’s brain injury at Fort Campbell Army hospital</div><span class="TeaserLink_galleryIcon__W3OjR"></span><span class="TeaserLink_videoIcon__vD91T"></span></div><div class="TeaserLink_teaserSummary__nulFo">A family who said their baby suffered a brain injury at a Fort Campbell Army hospital gets justice in an $11 million settlement with the federal government.</div></a><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TeaserLink_teaserLink__p1kuV TeaserLink_sidebarSecondary__CkQRW TeaserLink_updated__7BJDM" href="/news/local/87-year-old-driver-charged-after-deadly-broadway-crash?teaserSource=trending"><picture class="TeaserLink_teaserPicture__kW5wk"><source height="56" media="(max-width: 767px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/712/648/0x22/90/dafc2956-cf87-4c31-b52d-f646f5c53ee6-Screenshot20260904123515.png" width="100"/><source height="56" media="(min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/712/648/0x22/90/dafc2956-cf87-4c31-b52d-f646f5c53ee6-Screenshot20260904123515.png" width="100"/><img alt="" aria-hidden="true" fetchPriority="auto" height="100" loading="lazy" src="/resources/media2/1x1/443/200/135x0/90/dafc2956-cf87-4c31-b52d-f646f5c53ee6-Screenshot20260904123515.png" width="100"/></picture><div class="TeaserLink_teaserText__g1DQc"><div>87-year-old driver charged after deadly Broadway crash </div><span class="TeaserLink_galleryIcon__W3OjR"></span><span class="TeaserLink_videoIcon__vD91T"></span></div><div class="TeaserLink_teaserSummary__nulFo">Police have charged an 87-year-old Kentucky driver after a deadly crash at a busy Nashville crosswalk left one person dead and three others injured.</div></a><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TeaserLink_teaserLink__p1kuV TeaserLink_sidebarSecondary__CkQRW" href="/news/local/nashville-pedestrian-hit-by-tow-truck-while-crossing-intersection?teaserSource=trending"><picture class="TeaserLink_teaserPicture__kW5wk"><source height="56" media="(max-width: 767px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/856/648/5x0/90/ae4641ab-6f2a-4545-84a4-800b18b63ee0-TOWTRUCK.png" width="100"/><source height="56" media="(min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/856/648/5x0/90/ae4641ab-6f2a-4545-84a4-800b18b63ee0-TOWTRUCK.png" width="100"/><img alt="" aria-hidden="true" fetchPriority="auto" height="100" loading="lazy" src="/resources/media2/1x1/482/200/192x0/90/ae4641ab-6f2a-4545-84a4-800b18b63ee0-TOWTRUCK.png" width="100"/></picture><div class="TeaserLink_teaserText__g1DQc"><div>Nashville pedestrian hit by tow truck has been identified</div></div><div class="TeaserLink_teaserSummary__nulFo">An update has been released about a man who died after a tow truck hit him as he crossed the street.</div></a><div class="RightRailTrendingAd_nativeAdWrapper__dxc78"><div class="DisplayAd_Native__XCll3 native"><div><div id="ddb_fluid_native_ddb_0"></div></div></div></div><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TeaserLink_teaserLink__p1kuV TeaserLink_sidebarSecondary__CkQRW" href="/news/local/multi-vehicle-crash-blocks-2-lanes-on-i-24-west-in-davidson-county?teaserSource=trending"><picture class="TeaserLink_teaserPicture__kW5wk"><source height="56" media="(max-width: 767px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/1123/648/0x53/90/aab864a8-d520-494d-a18d-a3b790ca81ca-Screenshot20260904065829.png" width="100"/><source height="56" media="(min-width: 768px) and (max-width: 1023px)" srcSet="/resources/media2/16x9/1123/648/0x53/90/aab864a8-d520-494d-a18d-a3b790ca81ca-Screenshot20260904065829.png" width="100"/><img alt="" aria-hidden="true" fetchPriority="auto" height="100" loading="lazy" src="/resources/media2/1x1/737/200/193x0/90/aab864a8-d520-494d-a18d-a3b790ca81ca-Screenshot20260904065829.png" width="100"/></picture><div class="TeaserLink_teaserText__g1DQc"><div>Multi-vehicle crash blocks 2 lanes on I-24 West in Davidson County</div><span class="TeaserLink_galleryIcon__W3OjR"></span></div><div class="TeaserLink_teaserSummary__nulFo">As of 6:27 a.m. Thursday, a multi-vehicle crash was reported on Interstate 24 westbound in Davidson County.</div></a><a class="Link_link__1Aqdr TeaserLink_teaserLink__p1kuV TeaserLink_sidebarSecondary__CkQRW" href="/news/local/license-plate-reader-debate-grows-as-nashville-residents-weigh-safety-privacy?teaserSource=trending"><picture class="TeaserLink_teaserPicture__kW5wk"><source height="56" media="(max-wi